We establish quantitative inequalities controlling changes in squared edge lengths
by displacements of corresponding vertices. For two corresponding triangles, the
sharp uniform constant is 3/2. For corresponding closed n-gons in Euclidean spaces
of arbitrary finite dimension, a universal constant 2 is obtained without convexity
or nesting assumptions. We then prove cumulative estimates for m corresponding
layers, a first-to-last-layer estimate, and an m-round extension on a fixed finite graph.
The polygonal results are finally placed in the more general setting of finite undi-
rected simple graphs. We distinguish rigorously between a proved inequality con-
stant and the optimal constant: the sharpness of 3/2is established for triangles, whereas
the optimality of the upper bound 2 for fixed n ≥ 4 remains open.
